DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The iinniittttooddrr() function determines the time and sets the system clock.
     It tries to pick the correct time using a set of heuristics that examine
     the system's battery backed clock and the time obtained from the root
     file system, as given in _b_a_s_e.  How the _b_a_s_e value is obtained will vary
     depending on the root file system type.  The heuristics used include:

     ••   If the battery-backed clock has a valid time, it is used.

     ••   If the battery-backed clock does not have a valid time, the time pro‐
         vided in _b_a_s_e will be used.

     Once a system time has been determined, it is stored in the _t_i_m_e vari‐
     able.

DDIIAAGGNNOOSSTTIICCSS
     The iinniittttooddrr() function prints diagnostic messages if it has trouble fig‐
     uring out the system time.  Conditions that can cause diagnostic messages
     to be printed include:

     ••   The battery-backed clock's time appears nonsensical.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     resettodr(9), time(9)

BBUUGGSS
     On many systems, iinniittttooddrr() has to convert from a time expressed in terms
     of year, month, day, hours, minutes, and seconds to _t_i_m_e, expressed in
     seconds.  Many of the implementations could share code, but do not.

     Each system's heuristics for picking the correct time are slightly dif‐
     ferent.

     The FreeBSD implementation should do a better job of validating the time
     provided in _b_a_s_e when the battery-backed clock is unusable.  Currently it
     unconditionally sets the system clock to this value.

